{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Lift the head of, etc.</b> One needs to know — why this number? And Rashi, his memory for blessing, brought the words of the midrash that they said: for when He came to cause His Shekhinah to dwell among them, He returned to count them — thus far. And this does not suffice for resolving the plain sense, for He should have counted them before the month of Nisan, not afterward, and certainly not with such a great passage of a month's time." "And it appears to me, with the preliminary inquiry into this, that in the count after the calf — which is the count in parashat Pekudei (Exodus 38:26) — they reached a total of six hundred thousand and three thousand and five hundred and fifty. In this number there is an addition over the number of those who came out of Egypt of three thousand and five hundred and fifty, over the course of six months — even though the calf consumed among them three kinds of death: plague, the sword of the sons of Levi, and the drinking of the calf's ashes. And in the count before us, which is after six further months from the second count, it appears to me that they did not add to the previous number even one. And the matter is nearly extremely wondrous — that they did not add: how is it possible that during this period of time none of them reached the age of twenty? And all the more so since no diminution or increase befell them, for they were occupied with the work of the Tabernacle." "<b>And the difficulty grows greater</b> according to what they said in the Pesikta (Zutrata), and this is their language: ״וַיִּהְיוּ כָּל הַפְּקוּדִים״ etc., and further on it says ״בֶּקַע וְגוֹ׳ לְשֵׁשׁ מֵאוֹת אֶלֶף״ etc. — we learn that all the while that Israel were occupied with the work of the Tabernacle, not a single soul was lacking from them — thus far. Their words, their memory for blessing, appear remote, for is it not rather the contrary: by considering the matter we see that they diminished more than they diminished through the sin of the calf, even though they were not occupied with the work of the Tabernacle?" "<b>However,</b> according to what has come to our hands, that every tribe of Levi would weigh shekels (Shekalim 1:3), there is no difficulty — for in the count in parashat Pekudei the Levites were included in the number, but in the count before us the Levites were not included. From this point on, see that they did add the entire number of the Levites. And therefore it says in proximity to the count <b>but the Levites were not counted among the children of Israel</b>. And it should not have needed to say — as I see it — that they were not counted in the previous number; and it would be true if the verse said ״as HaShem commanded״ as the verse says after the parashah of the standards. [Then] I would say that it came to make known to us that they fulfilled the word of HaShem who commanded not to count them; but from the fact that it said only <b>but the Levites were not counted among them</b>, this points with a finger that it intended to say: do not wonder at the number, which is equal to the previous number without increase — <b>but the Levites were not counted</b>; and behold, the excess is before you." "<b>And the meaning</b> of the words of the Pesikta will blossom and shine, for the fact that we find such an astounding reckoning — that the second number is equal to the first — this declares that they, these very ones who were in the first count, during the six months between the two counts, not one of them was missing; and those added in the count filled the lack of the number of the Levites who were included in the first count but not in the second. And therefore HaShem showed wisdom in telling Moses to lift the head of the children of Israel on the first day of the second month etc., intending that this was the time when the count reached the first count minus the Levites, to alert you to what it said in the Pesikta: that the first count itself was like the second count, and not one person died and another entered in his place. And the reason: because they were occupied with the work of the Tabernacle, after the manner of (Deuteronomy 4:4) ״but you who cling to HaShem your Elohim — all of you are alive today.״ And we may also say, according to the words of the midrash that Rashi brought — that when He came to cause His Shekhinah to dwell among them etc. — that He chose this time for the reason mentioned."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "The last phrase seems textually difficult — it appears to say the diminishing happened 'even though they were not occupied with the work of the Tabernacle,' which cuts against the context. Translated hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:06.033700956Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}